  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Fig. 9, 'Example business process matrix from family planning DAK'. A table with the columns Process name, Process ID, Personas, Objectives and Task set, the header row defining each column. Three processes follow. A Registration (FP.A), with client and clerk or health-care provider, ensures the client is in the system and queued for counselling; its tasks are to search for, review and update, or create a client record. B Family planning counselling (FP.B), with client and health-care provider, discusses methods and selects one the client is medically eligible for; its tasks run from taking a history and a risk assessment through checking medical eligibility criteria and stock to referral if the facility cannot provide the method. C Service provision (FP.C) provides the chosen method, covering informed consent, start timing, dual protection and follow-up scheduling. Source: WHO (30).",
